Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 12:33:11 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/15] Repair what the adversarial review confirmed: the stale
 pin, c8, and the record floor

An independent reviewer attacked the re-applied patch series and the guard
change. Four findings are fixed here; each failed before and passes after.

F1, the branch was red. 9857556 ("opt-in rule_snapshots") edited
detect/detectors.py and re-pinned the report's SPIKE_CODE_HASH but not the
repo's DETECT_SHA256, so test_detection_is_byte_identical_to_its_pin has failed
since that commit: pinned 9d23000e..., file d113c269.... No run on this branch
could have had checks_pass true. Re-pinned deliberately, naming the commit and
the lines that changed it.

F2, the guard repair did not reach its own purpose. check_world_counts accepted
seeds 1-5, but c8 (report.py) still required every count of section 3.1's row,
so a run on any seed but 0 still failed its checks and could still only be
reported as a guard-bypass diagnostic. c8 now requires the seed-invariant counts
to equal the row and requires the draw to be *reported*: table_row_diff must be
present and may name only drawn counts. The split now has one definition, in
report.py, which stays importable without the generator; emergence_world imports
it back.

F9, the record floor was not derivable. "records >= users" is not a property of
the benchmark -- nothing says every user writes a record. It is now
MIN_SITES * ADMISSION_K * gold_discoverable (2 x 3 x 40 = 240 at 200 users),
which is the admission rule's own arithmetic: fewer records than that and a
planted pattern cannot be confirmable anywhere. The docstring no longer
understates what the repair gives up: the floors sit far below the draws the
generator makes (12,411-13,131 records), so a generator regression that cut the
corpus by an order of magnitude would now pass the guard, and table_row_diff
plus c8 are what catch it. The gold_rare bound is kept and labelled for what it
is -- a restatement that cannot fail from the generator, for hand-built counts.

F11, tests/mycelic/test_pushdown_n1.py ran unittest.main() above class N2Tests,
so the file's own entry point silently skipped the four N2 tests. Moved to the
end.

Evidence: tests/mycelic/test_collective_pushdown.py 1 failed -> 81 passed;
test_pushdown_n1.py 25 passed with all four N2 tests collected by __main__;
tests/e2e_ingest/test_emergence_world.py 24 passed, including a test that pins
the record floor to the admission rule's arithmetic.

Open from the same review, not fixed here: F3/F5 (near-duplicate roots are feed
order dependent and merge different readings), F4 (a sub-k sibling's refute does
not contest under N1), F6 (N2 constrains roots only), F7 (rule snapshots carry
supporting_sites from an earlier week), F13 (a site process gets demo_seed and
could regenerate Gold).

